The attached image illustrates that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response and is taken up by recruited cells at the injection site after intramuscular administration. Panel A quantifies inflammatory cells (neutrophils, Mac-2+ macrophages, antigen-presenting cells, eosinophils, and Ly6C+ monocytes) over 21 days, showing a peak in cell numbers, particularly monocytes/macrophages and antigen-presenting cells, around day 2, followed by a decline. Panel B shows confocal images confirming Nano-11 uptake by Mac-2+ monocytes/macrophages and MHCII+ cells at various time points, demonstrating efficient cellular internalization and retention of the nanoparticle-antigen complex. This localized and transient inflammation, predominantly involving phagocytic cells, contributes to Nano-11's strong adjuvant effect and favorable safety profile.